You are an adversarial reviewer of scientific figures for a research proposal. The attached PNGs are rendered at 2x from SVG and will be displayed about 850 px wide. Try hard to find reasons each figure should not ship. For EACH image, by file order, report: (a) overlapping or colliding text, (b) lines or arrows that cross text or other arrows confusingly, (c) arrows whose direction or endpoints are ambiguous, (d) text too small to read at display size, (e) excess text that belongs in a caption, (f) the one main point and whether a reader gets it in 5 seconds, (g) concrete fixes with coordinates or element names. Then give a verdict per figure: ship / fix / redraw. Focus: Proposal 07, 'Can a vision-language agent learn how to look at its own data?' (a VLM agent evolves the plotting code that renders its own biomechanics tool outputs; the question is whether gains transfer to other readers and people). Points, in file order: (1) one-output-four-encodings: one tandem-stance tool output (61,200 numbers) can reach the reader as printed numbers, summary statistics, a default plot or a stabilogram, each with a different token cost and different losses; (2) risk-decomposition: a reader's risk splits into tool floor R(o), information term I(e), and a perceptual term made of calibration and resolution, each boundary set by a different decoder; (3) oracle-learning-curve: preliminary simulation, with too few training renders the oracle is under-trained so the measured perceptual gap goes negative (-0.049 at 200 renders), and at 5,000 renders it nears the true gap; (4) renderer-evolution-loop: text-only mutator, frozen reader A, fitness on dev-select people, Pareto archive; sealed people, held-out readers and held-out tasks are scored only after the search ends; (5) reader-design-matrix: which readers set the fitness, which are held out within a family, which are unseen families or people; every condition leaves at least one VLM family out and no person scores renders during search; (6) transfer-decomposition-curve: illustrative, under single-reader fitness the reader-specific share widens over generations, under two-family ensemble fitness it stays narrow; (7) profile-predicts-transfer: illustrative, observed transfer near the diagonal of profile-predicted transfer, same-family high, other families and people lower; (8) co-adaptation-drift: illustrative, without a legibility term the co-adapted pair gains while fresh readers lose on the same renders (drift gap), with a frozen-reader term the gap stays small.. Do not run commands or modify files.

